From: Swordfish <swordfish@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] httpd: tell a handler when its client has disconnected

seastar's httpd gives a request handler no way to learn that the client has gone.
For an ordinary request that does not matter -- the reply is written and the
handler returns. For a LONG-LIVED body, written through reply::write_body(), it
matters a great deal: a peer that sends FIN leaves the socket writable, so writes
keep succeeding into a connection nobody is reading, and the handler runs until
the server is stopped.

In Swordfish this made the `http_server` OUTPUT lose data. Its `stream_path`
clients pop batches from one shared queue, so a handler whose client had died
kept taking batches and acknowledging them as delivered. Measured: with two
clients, one killed, the survivor received every other message and the pipeline
reported all of them acked. redpanda-connect does not have this problem because
Go's net/http cancels the request context on disconnect.

The information already exists inside seastar: connection::read() ends exactly
when the client's read side is gone, which is what sets `_done`. This patch only
makes that observable, in the smallest way we could find:

  * connection gains an abort_source, aborted once where the read loop ends --
    one hook covering every `_done = true` site, including the error paths.
  * request gains a pointer to it, `client_gone`, set by the server when it
    hands the parsed request over. It is null on a client-side request, which
    has no such thing.

Nothing existing changes behaviour: the abort_source is only signalled, and only
a handler that looks at `client_gone` can notice.

Upstreamable in principle. If a future seastar exposes an equivalent -- a
per-request abort source or a disconnect future -- drop this patch and use it.

--- a/include/seastar/http/request.hh
+++ b/include/seastar/http/request.hh
@@ -44,6 +44,8 @@
 
 namespace seastar {
 
+class abort_source;
+
 namespace http {
 
 class connection;
@@ -59,6 +61,18 @@
 
     socket_address _client_address;
     socket_address _server_address;
+    /// \brief Aborted when the client closes its end of the connection.
+    ///
+    /// Set by the HTTP server on a server-side request, and null otherwise (a
+    /// client-side request has no such thing). A handler writing a long-lived
+    /// response body -- a chunked stream, say -- otherwise has no way to learn
+    /// that its reader has gone: a peer that sends FIN leaves the socket
+    /// writable, so writes keep succeeding into a connection nobody is reading,
+    /// and the handler runs until the server is stopped.
+    ///
+    /// The server's read fiber already discovers this (it is what ends the read
+    /// loop and sets `_done`); this only makes the discovery observable.
+    abort_source* client_gone = nullptr;
     sstring _method;
     sstring _url;
     sstring _version;
--- a/include/seastar/http/httpd.hh
+++ b/include/seastar/http/httpd.hh
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 #include <vector>
 #include <optional>
 #include <boost/intrusive/list.hpp>
+#include <seastar/core/abort_source.hh>
 #include <seastar/http/request_parser.hh>
 #include <seastar/http/request.hh>
 #include <seastar/core/seastar.hh>
@@ -75,6 +76,11 @@
     // null element marks eof
     queue<std::unique_ptr<http::reply>> _replies { 10 };
     bool _done = false;
+    // Aborted when this connection's read side ends -- EOF from the client, or a
+    // read error. Handed to each request as `request::client_gone` so a handler
+    // writing a long-lived body can stop when its reader has gone; see the
+    // comment on that field.
+    abort_source _client_gone;
     const bool _tls;
 public:
     connection(http_server& server, connected_socket&& fd, bool tls)
--- a/src/http/httpd.cc
+++ b/src/http/httpd.cc
@@ -142,6 +142,13 @@
             _server._read_errors++;
         }
         f.ignore_ready_future();
+        // The read loop ends exactly when this connection's client is gone --
+        // EOF, or a read error -- whichever of the several `_done = true` sites
+        // fired. One hook here therefore covers all of them, including the error
+        // paths, and a handler still writing a body can observe it.
+        if (!_client_gone.abort_requested()) {
+            _client_gone.request_abort();
+        }
         return _replies.push_eventually( {});
     });
 }
@@ -204,6 +211,7 @@
 
         req->_server_address = this->_server_addr;
         req->_client_address = this->_client_addr;
+        req->client_gone = &this->_client_gone;
 
         if (_tls) {
             req->protocol_name = "https";
